gr-cyberether examples
======================

Open these in GNU Radio Companion. Every one of them needs the Options block's
"Generate Options" set to `cyberether_gui` — that is what lets CyberEther own
the main thread and open its window when you press Run. It is already set in
each file here.

Start with lineplot_demo.grc, then everything_demo.grc.

  lineplot_demo.grc
      The smallest useful flowgraph: a cosine into one line plot sink.
      Start here.

  waterfall_demo.grc
      One waterfall sink, with FFT size and history height exposed.

  constellation_demo.grc
      QPSK symbols with noise into the constellation sink.

  all_constellations_demo.grc
      BPSK, QPSK, 8-PSK and 16-QAM side by side in one window. Shows how
      several sinks share a mosaic.

  lineplot_types_and_device_demo.grc
      The same signal into a complex sink and a float sink, and how to pick
      the renderer device (Auto / CPU / CUDA / Metal / Vulkan / WebGPU).

  all_options_demo.grc
      Four plots with every block parameter set explicitly. Useful as a
      reference for what each knob does.

  everything_demo.grc
      The showcase: all three sink types, complex and float input, row and
      column spanning across a 3x3 mosaic, and two live sliders driving the
      flowgraph from inside the render window.


Layout
------

Sinks take a GUI Hint using the same syntax as the qtgui blocks:

    "row, col"                        one cell
    "row, col, row_span, col_span"    a rectangular block of cells

Sinks with no hint are placed automatically, and a malformed hint falls back
to auto-placement rather than failing the flowgraph.


A note on .py files
-------------------

Running a flowgraph in GRC writes a generated .py next to the .grc. Those are
build output, not source, and are gitignored — edit the .grc, never the .py.
